---
import Compare from "../components/Compare.astro";
---

<section id="collections">
  <h2>15. Arrays &amp; collections</h2>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Need</th><th>C#</th><th>TypeScript</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Resizable list</td><td><code>List&lt;T&gt;</code></td><td><code>T[]</code> / <code>Array&lt;T&gt;</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Dictionary</td><td><code>Dictionary&lt;K,V&gt;</code></td><td><code>Map&lt;K,V&gt;</code> or <code>Record&lt;K,V&gt;</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Set</td><td><code>HashSet&lt;T&gt;</code></td><td><code>Set&lt;T&gt;</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Queue / Stack</td><td><code>Queue&lt;T&gt;</code> / <code>Stack&lt;T&gt;</code></td><td><code>Array</code> as queue (<code>push/pop/shift</code>)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Immutable view</td><td><code>IReadOnlyList&lt;T&gt;</code> / <code>ImmutableList&lt;T&gt;</code></td><td><code>readonly T[]</code> / <code>Readonly&lt;T&gt;</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Lazy stream</td><td><code>IEnumerable&lt;T&gt;</code> + <code>yield</code></td><td><code>Iterable&lt;T&gt;</code> / generators <code>function*</code></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <Compare>
    <div slot="left">
      <pre><code>var nums = new List&lt;int&gt; &#123; 1, 2, 3 &#125;;
nums.Add(4);
int first = nums[0]; // throws if OOB

var byId = new Dictionary&lt;string, string&gt; &#123; ["1"] = "Ada" &#125;;
byId["2"] = "Grace";
bool has = byId.ContainsKey("1");
bool ok = byId.TryGetValue("1", out var name);

var tags = new HashSet&lt;string&gt; &#123; "a", "b" &#125;;
tags.Add("a"); // ignored duplicate

// slicing
int[] slice = nums.ToArray()[1..3]; // ranges</code></pre>
    </div>
    <div slot="right">
      <pre><code>const nums: number[] = [1, 2, 3];
nums.push(4);
const first = nums[0]; // number (or undefined with noUncheckedIndexedAccess)

const byId = new Map&lt;string, string&gt;([["1", "Ada"]]);
byId.set("2", "Grace");
const has = byId.has("1");
const name = byId.get("1"); // string | undefined

const byId2: Record&lt;string, string&gt; = &#123; "1": "Ada" &#125;;

const tags = new Set(["a", "b"]);
tags.add("a"); // ignored

// slicing (non-mutating)
const slice = nums.slice(1, 3);
const copy = [...nums];</code></pre>
    </div>
  </Compare>
  <ul>
    <li>C# indexers throw on missing keys; TS <code>Map.get</code> returns <code>undefined</code>. Handle it.</li>
    <li>Enable <code>noUncheckedIndexedAccess</code> so <code>arr[0]</code> is <code>T | undefined</code>.</li>
    <li>TS <code>Array</code> doubles as stack+queue. For O(1) queues use a deque lib; C# <code>Queue/Stack</code> are purpose-built.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

<hr />

<section id="linq">
  <h2>16. LINQ vs array methods</h2>
  <Compare>
    <div slot="left">
      <pre><code>var names = users
  .Where(u =&gt; u.Age &gt;= 18)
  .OrderBy(u =&gt; u.Name)
  .Select(u =&gt; u.Name)
  .ToList();

// query syntax (no TS equivalent)
var q = from u in users
        where u.Age &gt;= 18
        select u.Name;

int total = users.Sum(u =&gt; u.Age);
bool anyAdmin = users.Any(u =&gt; u.Role == "admin");
var dict = users.ToDictionary(u =&gt; u.Id);</code></pre>
    </div>
    <div slot="right">
      <pre><code>const names = users
  .filter((u) =&gt; u.age &gt;= 18)
  .sort((a, b) =&gt; a.name.localeCompare(b.name))
  .map((u) =&gt; u.name);
// no query syntax — chain methods

const total = users.reduce((s, u) =&gt; s + u.age, 0);
const anyAdmin = users.some((u) =&gt; u.role === "admin");
const allActive = users.every((u) =&gt; u.status === "active");
const dict = Object.fromEntries(users.map((u) =&gt; [u.id, u]));
const dictMap = new Map(users.map((u) =&gt; [u.id, u] as const));</code></pre>
    </div>
  </Compare>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>C# LINQ</th><th>TS equivalent</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>Where</code></td><td><code>filter</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>Select</code></td><td><code>map</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>SelectMany</code></td><td><code>flatMap</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>OrderBy/ThenBy</code></td><td><code>sort/toSorted</code> with comparator</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>First/FirstOrDefault</code></td><td><code>find</code> (<code>T | undefined</code>)</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>Any/All</code></td><td><code>some/every</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>Aggregate/Sum</code></td><td><code>reduce</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>Distinct</code></td><td><code>[...new Set(arr)]</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>Chunk/GroupBy</code></td><td>manual or <code>Object.groupBy</code> (ES2024)</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <h3>Lazy vs eager — performance deep dive</h3>
  <Compare>
    <div slot="left">
      <pre><code>// LINQ is lazy — nothing runs until enumerated
IEnumerable&lt;int&gt; q = nums.Where(n =&gt; &#123; Console.WriteLine(n); return n &gt; 1; &#125;);
// no output yet
var list = q.ToList(); // now it runs (deferred execution)</code></pre>
    </div>
    <div slot="right">
      <pre><code>// Array methods are eager — each step allocates
const out = nums.filter((n) =&gt; &#123; console.log(n); return n &gt; 1; &#125;);
// logs immediately
// For lazy pipelines, use generators:
function* lazyFilter&lt;T&gt;(xs: Iterable&lt;T&gt;, f: (t: T) =&gt; boolean): Generator&lt;T&gt; &#123;
  for (const x of xs) if (f(x)) yield x;
&#125;</code></pre>
    </div>
  </Compare>
  <ul>
    <li>TS <code>sort</code> mutates; use <code>toSorted/toReversed/toSpliced</code> for LINQ-like non-mutating behavior.</li>
    <li><code>Object.groupBy</code> is new — check target/lib before using, otherwise reduce into a <code>Map</code>.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

<hr />

<section id="strings">
  <h2>17. Strings, regex &amp; dates — in depth</h2>
  <Compare>
    <div slot="left">
      <pre><code>string msg = $"Hello, &#123;name&#125;!"; // interpolation
string upper = name.ToUpperInvariant();
string joined = string.Join(", ", new[] &#123; "a", "b" &#125;);
string[] parts = "a,b".Split(',');
bool starts = msg.StartsWith("Hello");
string sub = msg[0..5]; // ranges
string raw = @"C:\temp\file.txt"; // verbatim

// regex (compiled, typed groups in .NET 7+)
var m = Regex.Match("2024-01-02", @"(?&lt;y&gt;\d+)");
DateTime dt = DateTime.Parse("2024-01-02");</code></pre>
    </div>
    <div slot="right">
      <pre><code>const msg = `Hello, $&#123;name&#125;!`; // template literal
const upper = name.toUpperCase();
const joined = ["a", "b"].join(", ");
const parts = "a,b".split(",");
const starts = msg.startsWith("Hello");
const sub = msg.slice(0, 5);
const raw = String.raw`C:\temp\file.txt`;

// regex literals + named groups
const m = /(?&lt;y&gt;\d+)-(?&lt;mo&gt;\d+)/.exec("2024-01-02");
const y = m?.groups?.y;
const dt = new Date("2024-01-02"); // beware TZ parsing!
// prefer Temporal (polyfill) or date-fns for real work</code></pre>
    </div>
  </Compare>
  <ul>
    <li>C# <code>$""</code> ≈ TS backticks. C# verbatim <code>@""</code> ≈ <code>String.raw</code>.</li>
    <li>Date parsing: C# <code>DateOnly/TimeOnly/DateTimeOffset</code> are precise; TS <code>Date</code> is famously lossy — use ISO strings + a library.</li>
  </ul>
</section>
